(1113554, 134525, 19408, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by peecee:</b></div><hr/><i> <table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by dsb_mac:</b></div><hr/><i> that''s beautiful background boke. i wonder what lens you used. the subject is less interesting though. 8. </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks, canon 50mm 1.8 </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>ahh, I knew you had a good lens there!</td>', 'Originally posted by peecee: Originally posted by dsb_mac: that''s beautiful background boke. i wonder what lens you used. the subject is less interesting though. 8.   Thanks, canon 50mm 1.8   ahh, I knew you had a good lens there!', '2005-01-17 02:45:22.000000', NULL, 0),
(1113588, 134003, 30214, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by skiprow:</b></div><hr/><i> very interesting image.  i think you are on the right track, and that this image has a lot of promise.  with a subject that small, it really needs to knock the viewer''s socks off when the image is this large; unfortunately, there are so many other elements in the image competing for attention that your single droplet starts to lose its strength.  with such very strong bokeh all the way through, you could actually crop this image in half and have something that absolutely rocked.  try it:  hold your left hand up to cover the whole left half of the image and you''ll see what i mean.  anyways, this is good work.  good luck! </i></td></tr></table> <br/> <br/>Thanks all for great comments! I agree completely with a number of comments here that the white and dark spots in the left part are quite bit distracting. However, regarding cropping the left part out of this shot, - I thought about it, but decided against it because I loved the gentle curve of that stem. After such a cropping, it would''ve been just a diagonal line which is kind of boring compositionally.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-01-17 03:02:55.</i></td>', 'Originally posted by skiprow: very interesting image.  i think you are on the right track, and that this image has a lot of promise.  with a subject that small, it really needs to knock the viewer''s socks off when the image is this large; unfortunately, there are so many other elements in the image competing for attention that your single droplet starts to lose its strength.  with such very strong bokeh all the way through, you could actually crop this image in half and have something that absolutely rocked.  try it:  hold your left hand up to cover the whole left half of the image and you''ll see what i mean.  anyways, this is good work.  good luck!   Thanks all for great comments! I agree completely with a number of comments here that the white and dark spots in the left part are quite bit distracting. However, regarding cropping the left part out of this shot, - I thought about it, but decided against it because I loved the gentle curve of that stem. After such a cropping, it would''ve been just a diagonal line which is kind of boring compositionally.', '2005-01-17 03:02:20.000000', '2005-01-17 03:02:55.000000', 0),
(1113590, 134211, 23478, '<td valign="top">Thanks for all the comments... This shot was partly due to my misunderstanding of the challenge... I thought it stated "out of focus foreground <i>and </i>background...", not "or".  So, that explains a bit.<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-01-17 03:03:16.</i></td>', 'Thanks for all the comments... This shot was partly due to my misunderstanding of the challenge... I thought it stated "out of focus foreground and background...", not "or".  So, that explains a bit.', '2005-01-17 03:02:30.000000', '2005-01-17 03:03:16.000000', 0),
(1113593, 134475, 30364, '<td valign="top">Sadly underrated image.  I felt that one of its several strengths is the extent to which an otherwise unremarkable subject has been transformed by the impact of the bokeh.  I was floundering about trying to work out what this challenge was really about, until I saw this photograph.  Many that rated higher were merely good photos of intrinsically interesting subjects, with a bit of blurry stuff included to "meet" the challenge. Moreover, I feel this photo has additional compositional qualities that go beyond this challenge.   Finally, what a bonus to discover a Kiwi took it!</td>', 'Sadly underrated image.  I felt that one of its several strengths is the extent to which an otherwise unremarkable subject has been transformed by the impact of the bokeh.  I was floundering about trying to work out what this challenge was really about, until I saw this photograph.  Many that rated higher were merely good photos of intrinsically interesting subjects, with a bit of blurry stuff included to "meet" the challenge. Moreover, I feel this photo has additional compositional qualities that go beyond this challenge.   Finally, what a bonus to discover a Kiwi took it!', '2005-01-17 03:05:37.000000', NULL, 0),
(1113598, 134482, 23458, '<td valign="top">Well this is an interesting subject - and I really like the effect of the lighting here. I think this image suffered in terms of comments because of the number of people that gave it fives!! I''ve never seen a graph so heavily centred before !</td>', 'Well this is an interesting subject - and I really like the effect of the lighting here. I think this image suffered in terms of comments because of the number of people that gave it fives!! I''ve never seen a graph so heavily centred before !', '2005-01-17 03:11:00.000000', NULL, 0),
(1113621, 134816, 19408, '<td valign="top">it looks as if you used an actual guitar for the background. A pretty thoughtful and unnoticed touch if it is the case. To get a more blurry background effect try placing whatever you want blurry much further behind the object in focus (in this case the blue object behind your red guitar). I don''t know if your camera has manual aperture settings but if it does the larger the aperture ''A'' (smaller the number) you set the easier it is to blur things. If you can''t set it manually I think a trick is to use "night" mode and the camera should set itself automatically to its largest aperture thus achieving the same effect. The blur effect is often used to make your picture''s main subject stand out which in turn usually makes a better photograph. Try it out you may find it to your liking! Good work!<br/><br/><i>Message edited by author 2005-01-22 08:35:26.</i></td>', 'it looks as if you used an actual guitar for the background. A pretty thoughtful and unnoticed touch if it is the case. To get a more blurry background effect try placing whatever you want blurry much further behind the object in focus (in this case the blue object behind your red guitar). I don''t know if your camera has manual aperture settings but if it does the larger the aperture ''A'' (smaller the number) you set the easier it is to blur things. If you can''t set it manually I think a trick is to use "night" mode and the camera should set itself automatically to its largest aperture thus achieving the same effect. The blur effect is often used to make your picture''s main subject stand out which in turn usually makes a better photograph. Try it out you may find it to your liking! Good work!', '2005-01-17 03:31:14.000000', '2005-01-22 08:35:26.000000', 0),
